1
00:00:00,014 --> 00:00:03,600
Here's a strange fact about large language models.

2
00:00:03,950 --> 00:00:13,840
They can write essays and code from just a few examples, yet they struggle with basic things, like dividing 400 by 1400.

3
00:00:14,190 --> 00:00:22,308
A pocket calculator, a tiny program, never gets that wrong, so why not let the language model simply ask one?

4
00:00:23,182 --> 00:00:29,796
That's the idea behind Toolformer, a 2023 paper from Meta AI Research.

5
00:00:30,190 --> 00:00:36,420
Its title makes a bold claim: Language Models Can Teach Themselves to Use Tools.

6
00:00:37,294 --> 00:00:46,212
We'll see how the model writes its own training data, how it judges which tool calls are worth keeping, and how well the result works.

7
00:00:47,310 --> 00:00:54,480
The paper begins with a list of things language models are bad at, problems that more scale only partly fixes.

8
00:00:54,862 --> 00:00:58,788
They don't know about recent events, and they tend to make facts up.

9
00:00:59,214 --> 00:01:04,720
They struggle with rare languages, with precise math, and with the passage of time.

10
00:01:05,070 --> 00:01:08,160
Each of these has a simple fix outside the model.

11
00:01:08,462 --> 00:01:10,640
A search engine knows the facts.

12
00:01:10,990 --> 00:01:18,692
A translator handles rare languages, a calculator does arithmetic, and a calendar knows today's date.

13
00:01:19,566 --> 00:01:33,520
Earlier systems did teach models to use tools, but they either needed large amounts of human annotation, or they worked only within one task, where the prompt shows exactly which tool to use and how.

14
00:01:34,638 --> 00:01:36,560
Toolformer sets two goals.

15
00:01:36,846 --> 00:01:42,372
First, learn tool use in a self-supervised way without a big annotation effort.

16
00:01:42,766 --> 00:01:49,920
Second, stay a general language model, one that decides for itself when and how to use which tool.

17
00:01:50,254 --> 00:01:57,348
And what humans find useful may differ from what a model finds useful, so let the model be the judge.

18
00:01:58,446 --> 00:02:01,600
First, how can a language model use a tool at all?

19
00:02:01,902 --> 00:02:07,556
All it can do is produce text, so a tool call has to be written as text too.

20
00:02:08,878 --> 00:02:13,028
A call has two parts, the tool's name and its input.

21
00:02:13,358 --> 00:02:20,240
Written out, that's the name with the input in parentheses wrapped between special start and end markers.

22
00:02:20,526 --> 00:02:26,880
Once the tool answers, its result R goes in after an arrow just before the end marker.

23
00:02:27,214 --> 00:02:34,480
In practice, the markers are just square brackets, and the arrow is a dash followed by a greater than sign.

24
00:02:34,862 --> 00:02:38,052
Nothing new is added to the model's vocabulary.

25
00:02:38,990 --> 00:02:41,412
Here's one of the paper's own examples.

26
00:02:41,806 --> 00:02:53,120
Partway through a sentence, the model calls the calculator with 400 divided by 1400, gets back 0.29, and then writes 29%.

27
00:02:54,638 --> 00:03:05,680
The paper gives the model five tools: a question-answering system, a Wikipedia search engine, a calculator, a calendar, and a machine translation system.

28
00:03:06,190 --> 00:03:09,444
Every input and every output is plain text.

29
00:03:10,510 --> 00:03:17,444
Now for the central trick start with a big pile of ordinary text, with no tool calls in it at all.

30
00:03:17,902 --> 00:03:23,280
The goal is to turn it into the same text, with useful tool calls sprinkled in.

31
00:03:23,566 --> 00:03:31,364
This happens in three steps sample candidate calls, execute them, then filter out the ones that don't help.

32
00:03:32,654 --> 00:03:34,400
Take this sentence from the paper.

33
00:03:34,702 --> 00:03:37,668
Pittsburgh is also known as the Steel City.

34
00:03:37,998 --> 00:03:41,760
Partway through, the model proposes questions it could ask.

35
00:03:42,094 --> 00:03:46,788
What other name is Pittsburgh known by, or which country is Pittsburgh in?

36
00:03:47,054 --> 00:03:51,120
Executing them gives Steel City and United States.

37
00:03:51,470 --> 00:03:55,920
The first answer makes the rest of the sentence much easier to predict.

38
00:03:56,238 --> 00:04:00,320
The second doesn't help at all, so only the first survives.

39
00:04:00,718 --> 00:04:07,200
The surviving call is written into the text, and the model is then fine-tuned on text like this.

40
00:04:07,534 --> 00:04:09,252
Let's go through each step.

41
00:04:10,350 --> 00:04:14,020
Step 1 uses the model's own in-context learning.

42
00:04:14,382 --> 00:04:24,000
For each tool, the authors write a prompt, a short instruction, plus a few examples of text with calls inserted in sensible places.

43
00:04:24,398 --> 00:04:27,280
At the end goes the new piece of text, X.

44
00:04:27,790 --> 00:04:31,940
The model's job is to copy X, adding calls of its own.

45
00:04:32,814 --> 00:04:42,160
Now at every position I in X, we can ask the model, how likely is it that you'd start an API call right here, just before word i?

46
00:04:42,702 --> 00:04:46,960
That's PI, the probability of the a PI token at that spot.

47
00:04:47,246 --> 00:04:51,680
Positions where it beats a threshold, tau s, become candidates.

48
00:04:52,078 --> 00:04:59,460
By default, tau s is 5%, and at most the top K positions are kept, with K equal to 5.

49
00:05:00,526 --> 00:05:11,040
At each kept position, the model then writes out up to M complete calls, also 5 by default, stopping each one when it produces the end marker.

50
00:05:11,310 --> 00:05:13,280
Nothing has been checked yet.

51
00:05:13,614 --> 00:05:17,700
These are only the model's guesses about where a tool might help.

52
00:05:18,766 --> 00:05:20,708
Step 2 is the simple one.

53
00:05:20,960 --> 00:05:25,680
Run every call and collect each result as a single piece of text.

54
00:05:26,094 --> 00:05:33,680
The question-answering tool is Atlas, a retrieval-augmented language model fine-tuned on natural questions.

55
00:05:34,062 --> 00:05:40,388
Wikipedia search is a classic BM25 retriever that returns short snippets.

56
00:05:40,750 --> 00:05:49,200
The calculator is a small Python script that knows the four basic operations and rounds to two decimal places.

57
00:05:49,550 --> 00:05:51,840
The calendar takes no input at all.

58
00:05:52,142 --> 00:05:54,320
It just returns today's date.

59
00:05:54,702 --> 00:06:05,348
And the translator is NLLB, a 600 million parameter model covering 200 languages, which always translates into English.

60
00:06:06,414 --> 00:06:08,800
Step 3 is the heart of the paper.

61
00:06:09,134 --> 00:06:14,560
Without any human in the loop, how do you decide whether a tool call was useful?

62
00:06:14,862 --> 00:06:17,040
The answer is beautifully simple.

63
00:06:17,390 --> 00:06:22,180
A call is useful if it helps the model predict the text that comes next.

64
00:06:23,470 --> 00:06:24,640
Here's the measure.

65
00:06:25,006 --> 00:06:34,080
Li of Z is the model's loss on the tokens from position I onward when it's given some prefix Z, such as a tool call.

66
00:06:34,478 --> 00:06:38,160
Lower loss means the upcoming words were easier to guess.

67
00:06:38,510 --> 00:06:41,476
The weights favor the tokens right after the call.

68
00:06:41,838 --> 00:06:46,800
They fall by 0.2 per token and hit zero after five tokens.

69
00:06:47,182 --> 00:06:51,556
So a call has to help nearby, not somewhere far down the page.

70
00:06:52,430 --> 00:07:00,960
Now compare three situations, no call at all, the call but without its answer, and the call together with its answer.

71
00:07:01,294 --> 00:07:05,120
Compute the weighted loss on the upcoming words in each case.

72
00:07:05,486 --> 00:07:09,092
L plus is the loss with the full call and its answer.

73
00:07:09,518 --> 00:07:12,720
L--minus is the better of the two alternatives.

74
00:07:13,102 --> 00:07:21,120
The call is kept only if L-L plus is at least tau F, a threshold that is won by default.

75
00:07:21,454 --> 00:07:23,840
Why include the call without its answer?

76
00:07:24,206 --> 00:07:29,600
Because then the credit has to go to the tool's response, not just to asking the question.

77
00:07:29,966 --> 00:07:34,148
Here the gain is 1.6, so this call is kept.

78
00:07:34,990 --> 00:07:45,732
The other candidate, which country is Pittsburgh in, gets an answer that says nothing about the next words, so it falls below the threshold and is thrown away.

79
00:07:46,830 --> 00:07:49,792
Does this score really pick out sensible calls?

80
00:07:50,126 --> 00:07:54,752
The paper shows real examples from its data, sorted by score.

81
00:07:55,054 --> 00:08:05,252
At the top, scoring 5.49, a Wikipedia search about the Flodden window returns exactly the history the text goes on to describe.

82
00:08:05,742 --> 00:08:11,152
A question about the Nile's length returns the very number the text states next.

83
00:08:11,534 --> 00:08:22,272
At the bottom are calls that tell the model nothing, like a date lookup before a story about Disneyland or a garbled question, who was last time I was with.

84
00:08:22,638 --> 00:08:23,792
It isn't perfect.

85
00:08:24,078 --> 00:08:30,432
A search for fast train success returns an unrelated song, yet still lowers the loss.

86
00:08:30,798 --> 00:08:33,792
The authors argue a little noise is even useful.

87
00:08:34,126 --> 00:08:37,952
It teaches the model not to blindly trust every result.

88
00:08:38,990 --> 00:08:40,672
How many examples survive?

89
00:08:41,070 --> 00:08:48,992
With Tau F at 1, about 61,000 for Wikipedia search, but fewer than a thousand for the calculator.

90
00:08:49,486 --> 00:08:55,268
Raise the threshold to two, and only 138 calculator examples remain.

91
00:08:56,366 --> 00:09:03,392
After filtering, each surviving call is inserted into its text, right at the position where it was proposed.

92
00:09:03,790 --> 00:09:07,792
Doing this for every document gives the new dataset C star.

93
00:09:08,494 --> 00:09:10,372
And here's the crucial property.

94
00:09:10,766 --> 00:09:24,260
Apart from the inserted calls, CSTAR contains exactly the same text as the original data, so fine-tuning on it teaches tool use without changing what the model learns about language.

95
00:09:25,518 --> 00:09:38,660
In the experiments, the model is GPTJ, with 6.7 billion parameters, trained with plain next token prediction on a subset of CCNet, a large web crawl.

96
00:09:39,566 --> 00:09:42,112
At inference time, the model just writes.

97
00:09:42,542 --> 00:09:46,112
When it produces the arrow token, decoding pauses.

98
00:09:46,446 --> 00:09:53,632
The real tool is called, its answer is pasted in along with the end marker, and the model carries on writing.

99
00:09:53,966 --> 00:09:56,032
Two tweaks for the evaluations.

100
00:09:56,430 --> 00:10:11,940
The model may start a call whenever the API token is among its ten most likely next tokens, not only when it's the single top choice, and it gets at most one call per input, so it can't get stuck calling tools forever.

101
00:10:13,038 --> 00:10:14,512
On to the experiments.

102
00:10:14,798 --> 00:10:16,592
Every test is zero shot.

103
00:10:17,038 --> 00:10:22,832
The model gets an instruction, but no examples, and nobody tells it which tool to use.

104
00:10:23,182 --> 00:10:38,832
Six models are compared: GPTJ, GPTJ fine-tuned on the same text without calls, toolformer with its tools switched off, toolformer itself, and two far larger models, OPT and GPT-3.

105
00:10:39,950 --> 00:10:45,712
First LAMA, complete a short statement with a missing fact, like a date or a place.

106
00:10:46,190 --> 00:10:51,232
Wikipedia search is switched off here because Lama was built from Wikipedia.

107
00:10:51,598 --> 00:11:02,820
Toolformer beats the best same-size baseline by 11.7, 5.2, and 18.6 points, and it beats GPT3 on all three.

108
00:11:03,278 --> 00:11:08,580
It asked the question-answering tool in 98.1% of cases.

109
00:11:09,486 --> 00:11:15,232
Next, math word problems from three benchmarks are s-div, svamp, and morps.

110
00:11:15,630 --> 00:11:17,392
Here the gap is even bigger.

111
00:11:17,710 --> 00:11:24,592
With tools allowed, toolformer more than doubles its own score and lands far above GPT3.

112
00:11:25,230 --> 00:11:29,792
It called the calculator for 97.9% of examples.

113
00:11:30,126 --> 00:11:34,352
Even with tools disabled, it beats plain GPTJ.

114
00:11:34,766 --> 00:11:40,432
The authors suspect all those calculator results improved its own arithmetic.

115
00:11:41,614 --> 00:11:48,772
For open question answering, the question answering tool is disabled, since it would make the task trivial.

116
00:11:49,134 --> 00:11:55,024
So Toolformer leans on Wikipedia search 99.3% of the time.

117
00:11:55,406 --> 00:12:00,544
It beats every GPT-J model but falls short of GPT-3.

118
00:12:01,230 --> 00:12:08,836
The authors blame their simple search engine and Toolformer's inability to rephrase a query or browse results.

119
00:12:09,710 --> 00:12:15,104
On multilingual question answering, calling the translator helps in every language.

120
00:12:15,502 --> 00:12:24,516
But Toolformer doesn't consistently beat plain GPT-J because fine-tuning on CCNet had already hurt some languages.

121
00:12:25,422 --> 00:12:26,384
Finally, time.

122
00:12:26,990 --> 00:12:32,304
On dateset, a new set of questions like, What day of the week was it 30 days ago?

123
00:12:32,686 --> 00:12:40,292
Toolformer jumps to 27.3%, using the calendar for 54.8% of examples.

124
00:12:40,654 --> 00:12:42,624
No other model reaches 6.

125
00:12:42,990 --> 00:12:50,544
On a second dataset, Temp Lama, Toolformer also comes out ahead, but it almost never uses the calendar there.

126
00:12:50,990 --> 00:12:54,980
The gains came from search and question answering instead.

127
00:12:56,078 --> 00:12:58,544
Did learning to use tools cost anything?

128
00:12:58,862 --> 00:13:02,704
Perplexity measures how well a model predicts ordinary text.

129
00:13:03,024 --> 00:13:04,304
Lower is better.

130
00:13:04,654 --> 00:13:17,824
With calls disabled, Toolformer's Perplexity matches GPT-J fine-tuned on the same text without calls, 10.3 on WikiText and 10.5 on CCNet.

131
00:13:18,286 --> 00:13:20,388
Learning the tools came for free.

132
00:13:21,294 --> 00:13:23,584
Does this work for smaller models too?

133
00:13:23,982 --> 00:13:35,904
The authors repeated the whole recipe with four GPT2 models, from 124 million to 1.6 billion parameters, using three of the tools.

134
00:13:36,270 --> 00:13:42,532
The ability to use tools only emerges at around 775 million parameters.

135
00:13:42,926 --> 00:13:47,652
Below that, the curves with and without tools sit close together.

136
00:13:48,046 --> 00:13:53,024
Above it, they split, and the gap stays large even for the biggest model.

137
00:13:53,326 --> 00:14:04,452
The exception is Wikipedia search on the question-answering benchmarks, which helps even the small models, probably because it's comparatively easy to use.

138
00:14:05,518 --> 00:14:07,024
Remember the top K trick.

139
00:14:07,406 --> 00:14:18,944
With plain greedy decoding, k equal to 1, Toolformer calls a tool for only 40.3% of T-Rex questions and 8.5% on Web QS.

140
00:14:19,598 --> 00:14:25,024
At K equal to 10, that rises to 98.1 and 100.

141
00:14:25,358 --> 00:14:28,772
At K equal to 1, the model is somewhat calibrated.

142
00:14:29,070 --> 00:14:32,624
It calls tools on the examples it would get wrong on its own.

143
00:14:33,038 --> 00:14:40,484
The T-Rex examples its skips score 44.3, well above the 34.9 of never calling.

144
00:14:40,910 --> 00:14:43,940
At higher K, that calibration is lost.

145
00:14:44,846 --> 00:14:47,344
The authors are upfront about the limits.

146
00:14:47,758 --> 00:14:57,264
Toolformer can't chain tools, feeding one tool's output into another, because every call in its training data was sampled independently.

147
00:14:57,614 --> 00:15:01,664
It can't use a tool interactively, like refining a search.

148
00:15:01,998 --> 00:15:05,092
It's sensitive to the exact wording of its input.

149
00:15:05,454 --> 00:15:15,652
It's sample inefficient, over a million documents yield only a few thousand useful calculator calls, and it ignores how expensive a call is.

150
00:15:16,750 --> 00:15:27,780
So to recap, the model annotates ordinary text with candidate tool calls, runs them, and keeps only the calls that make the next words easier to predict.

151
00:15:28,174 --> 00:15:31,012
Then it learns from its own filtered data.

152
00:15:31,950 --> 00:15:46,532
The result is a 6.7 billion parameter model that, in zero-shot tests, often beats GPT-3, which is about 25 times larger, without giving up its language modeling ability.

153
00:15:47,406 --> 00:15:57,184
Looking back, tool use soon became a standard feature of AI assistance, which now routinely calls search, code execution, and other services.

154
00:15:57,614 --> 00:16:07,172
Toolformer, presented at Newer IPS 2023, is one of the most widely cited early papers behind that idea.

